  "sections": {
    "work": [
      {
        "kind": "quote",
        "text": "The affected pose may have been the inevitable result of the experiment Caravaggio appears to have been undertaking here: observing and recording acute emotions – surprise and fear – in a situation where real surprise was impossible and where the pose had to be held for a considerable period.",
        "source": {
          "author": "Wikipedia contributors",
          "title": "Boy Bitten by a Lizard",
          "readAt": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boy_Bitten_by_a_Lizard",
          "accessed": "2026-11-04",
          "tier": 5,
          "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "quote",
        "text": "Boy Bitten by a Lizard is an important work in the artist's early oeuvre precisely because it shows a way out from the airless stillness of very early works such as Boy Peeling a Fruit and Sick Bacchus",
        "source": {
          "author": "Wikipedia contributors",
          "title": "Boy Bitten by a Lizard",
          "readAt": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boy_Bitten_by_a_Lizard",
          "accessed": "2026-11-04",
          "tier": 5,
          "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
        }
      }
    ],
    "moment": [
      {
        "kind": "quote",
        "text": "As was first suggested by Roberto Longhi, Caravaggio has probably borrowed the motif of biting a finger from a Boy Bitten by a Crab, a drawing by prominent Renaissance artist Sofonisba Anguissola.",
        "source": {
          "author": "Wikipedia contributors",
          "title": "Boy Bitten by a Lizard",
          "readAt": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boy_Bitten_by_a_Lizard",
          "accessed": "2026-11-04",
          "tier": 5,
          "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "quote",
        "text": "Both versions are thought to date from the period 1594–1596.",
        "source": {
          "author": "Wikipedia contributors",
          "title": "Boy Bitten by a Lizard",
          "readAt": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boy_Bitten_by_a_Lizard",
          "accessed": "2026-11-04",
          "tier": 5,
          "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
        }
      }
    ],
    "matters": [
      {
        "kind": "quote",
        "text": "The scene might refer to the pain that can derive from love – a warning against the perils of love and the unexpected dangers that lurk behind beautiful things.",
        "source": {
          "author": "National Gallery, London",
          "title": "Boy bitten by a Lizard",
          "readAt": "https://www.nationalgallery.org.uk/paintings/michelangelo-merisi-da-caravaggio-boy-bitten-by-a-lizard",
          "accessed": "2026-11-04",
          "tier": 4
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "quote",
        "text": "Michael Fried has proposed instead that the painting is a disguised self-portrait of Caravaggio.",
